 but  what we are doing right now and there's  a tremendous amount of pressure since  the ukrainian government advised people  yesterday to evacuate immediately a  number of those areas donetsk luhansk uh  even kharkiv is that we are receiving a  lot of calls for our medical referral  train to come and pick up patients from  hospitals that are overburdened in the  east and take those patients transport  them or transfer them to hospitals that  are better able to provide them a safe  place to be able to be treated and  recover  so that train activity the medical  referral train is is now under a lot of  pressure with many many requests and far  too many for us to be able to safely  transport which is why we're developing  a larger train that's kitted up with  more equipment more like an intensive  care unit to be able to to assist  greater numbers of people and make such  a difference for the working flow of  of the hospital uh workload in the east  abril thank you very much for speaking  with us that was abriel benoit from  doctors without borders speaking to us  from libya in western ukraine  ministers are meeting in brussels today  to consider the western alliance's  response to the war in ukraine amid  worries that the conflict could drag on  for months if not years ukrainian  foreign minister dimitra koleba is  attending that meeting just a short  while ago he and nato chief jens  stoltenberg addressed reporters  it is an urgent need to further support  ukraine  and at our meeting later on with the  nato uh foreign ministers i'm certain  that we will address the need for  more air defense systems anti-tank  weapons lighter but also heavier  weapons and many different types of  support to ukraine my agenda is very  simple it has only three items on it  it's weapons  weapons and weapons  we are confident that the best way  to help ukraine now is to provide it  with all necessary  to contain  putin  and to defeat russian army in ukraine  our correspondent jack parrik is  covering that nato meeting in brussels  jack ukraine wants weapons jen  stoltenberg the nato chief says he  expects nato will deliver so are the two  sides on the same page  well  kind of to be honest terry it looks like  dimitro collabor is going to do a pretty  impassioned plea to the nato foreign  ministers through these meetings he said  some really interesting things in this  press conference he  pointed the finger  directly at berlin for not providing as  many weapons as they wanted he said  berlin has time kiev doesn't saying that  the bureaucracy in berlin to get weapons  over the line and to be sending them to  ukraine uh is taking too long he said  that the the decision by germany to  export weapons was a revolutionary step  that called on germany specifically to  do more he also talked about the  difference between defensive and  offensive weapons because that's some of  the discussion around some of the nato  member states and also specifically in  germany and culebra had to say that all  weapons right now are defensive because  they have been invaded um by russia and  there's no longer an offensive weapon  for them they see offensive weapons  simply as weapons that they  need to use to to push that invasion out  he also talked specifically about the  sort of some of the thinking around this  because nato members are slightly  concerned about sending uh weapons to  ukraine  for fear of being dragged into the war  in a more broader sense but what labour  had to say is give there's a simple deal  from the ukrainian side  give us weapons he said we sacrifice our  lives and we prevent the fallout from  this war going out into other european  countries so it was a pretty specific  set of demands he is here in brussels to  try and get weapons from the nato allies  nato's secretary general says we should  be prepared for the long haul in ukraine  is that not an admission jack that  nato's hands are effectively tied in  this conflict  well it's an interesting one isn't it  terry i mean there's there's this  preparation now i think and jen  stoltenberg has been talking over the  last few weeks about this this idea of  the new normal the troops that they've  stationed in eastern nato member states  that they're saying uh you know need to  um  you know are probably likely going to be  a more permanent position now if this  war continues on and rumbles on the  question is how does the nato thinking  shift on this and i think actually what  annalina bairbach the german foreign  minister uh said in response to what  kulaiba was saying regarding german arms  she was talking about the sort of  feelings that europe has and and the  sort of sense of of of what's going on  and what they need to try and do and and  i think there are some shifts and some  discussions happening within the german  government although she was relatively  vague in her response to what ku labo  was saying with the demands for weapons  jack there's mounting evidence of war  crimes being committed by russian forces  in ukraine is this creating a greater  sense of urgency among nato and eu  leaders there in brussels  i think definitely what we've seen over  the last seven days or so there's been  some pretty pretty big statements coming  out of eu leaders we also know that the  ambassadors of the european union are  sitting back down today to try and  see if they can agree on this fifth  round of eu sanctions including an  all-out ban on imports of russian coal  we'll have to wait and see how that goes  there are it seems there is some  stalling as well but i think there is  some impotence impetus after those  atrocities that we saw in butcher  jack thank you very much our  correspondent jack parrik there in  brussels
 

